making a direct sequel to a movie  decades later is becoming more common in  Hollywood recently it presents a unique  challenge for directors and  cinematographers as they have to find  the balance between creating something  that feels connected to the original  film while still creating something that  is unique and new from it was usually I  make my movies are here is Denny  Villeneuve the director of Blade Runner  2049 describing his relationship to the  original Blade Runner this is the first  time I was making a movie inspired by  another movie and I didn't try to stay  away from it I just kept it as a Bible  as a reference as as a music that I was  very close to me that I area  I was always referring to him every time  I was directing everyday thinking about  the spirit of the first movie  denne and director of photography Roger  Deakins developed a unique visual style  updating the world and the production  design for a story that takes place long  after the events of the original film  Roger Deakins often abandons the hard  high-contrast lighting of the original  for his more signature soft and diffused  lighting style production design has  been updated to reflect the changes in  the world  but there are still visual connections  the ones I've highlighted here ground  2049 in a familiar space reoccurring  places feel similar and visual motifs  are used to link past and present  characters and themes threading the  needle of being original while remaining  true to the source material must be a  difficult task I think where 2049  succeeds is by making these references  subtly enough that it doesn't feel  formulaic or forced most of the visual  references are made during segments of  the film that are establishing or  transitioning shots  20:49 doesn't lean on these references  to be the weight of the story instead it  uses them to allude to a similar place  and world while building towards a story  that is fresh and new  I'm Thomas light thank you so much for  taking the time to check out this video  in my channel as always a quick thank  you to my patreon supporters they helped  me continue making these videos I often  get asked where I get the music I use in  my videos I want to tell you very  quickly about artless then using art  list for well over a year artless has an  excellent high-quality music library you  only pay an annual subscription fee and  you get unlimited access to the tracks  in the library click the link in the  description to browse the library and  preview some of the tracks and if you  decide sign up it helps support my  channel thanks again for watching hit  that subscribe button or that share  button and I'll talk to you again soon  [Music]
 

